<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The relying-party association (spec 019 T138).

	  Without this entry the passkey provider refuses every ceremony for
	  getvela.app, so the app cannot create OR use a wallet — the whole feature
	  is inert. The file was empty until this feature checked it, which is
	  exactly the kind of gap that only shows up on a device.

	  The other half already exists and was verified live: the
	  apple-app-site-association file on the production domain returns 200 as
	  application/json and lists this app under `webcredentials`:

	      {"webcredentials":{"apps":["F9W689P9NE.app.getvela.VelaWallet", …]}}

	  Both halves must name the same domain. `webcredentials:` is what passkeys
	  read; `applinks:` is the separate association the /sign deep link uses, and
	  it is listed in the same AASA file.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>webcredentials:getvela.app</string>
		<string>applinks:getvela.app</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Smart-card access, for the app-owned CTAP2-over-CCID path (spec 019 T171b).

	  CryptoTokenKit's `TKSmartCard` is the ONLY way a third-party iOS app can
	  talk to an external security key over USB — iOS exposes no USB-HID host
	  API. Without this entitlement `TKSmartCardSlotManager.default` is nil and
	  the app-owned USB-C security-key route does not exist. Unlike the
	  associated-domain above, this path consults NO domain association: it is
	  the escape hatch a lapsed or down assetlinks server cannot padlock
	  (FR-009c). Needs a YubiKey with firmware 5.8+ (FIDO over CCID).

	  Spec 088 (2026-10-01): this key never reaches a SIGNED binary. Xcode drops
	  it when signing for iOS — it is absent from the archive's .xcent and from
	  the App Store export's entitlements, and no provisioning profile for this
	  App ID grants it — so it does not break archiving or export, and every
	  signed build (Debug on a device, TestFlight, App Store) runs without it.
	  Whatever the USB security-key route does on a device today is what it
	  does in TestFlight. Kept, because it costs nothing and documents intent.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.smartcard</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
